, rising to 3,238 metres on the border of the cantons of Obwalden and Bern.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tito-okello"><span class="enc-name">Tito Okello</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tito Okello was a Ugandan army officer who became head of state after the coup of July 1985 that ousted Milton Obote.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tito-venier"><span class="enc-name">Tito Venier</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tito Venier was a Venetian noble of Crete who in 1363 led the revolt of the island's Venetian colonists against Venice, sparked by a new tax for the harbour of Candia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tito-winyi-iv"><span class="enc-name">Tito Winyi IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tito Winyi IV was omukama of Bunyoro from 1924 until 1967.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titterstone-clee-hill"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titterstone-clee-hill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titterstone Clee Hill</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Titterstone Clee Hill is a hill in south Shropshire, England, rising to 533 metres.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titu-cusi-yupanqui"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titu-cusi-yupanqui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titu Cusi Yupanqui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titu Cusi Yupanqui (1529–1571) was ruler of the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba from 1563, a son of Manco Inca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titus was Roman emperor from 79 to 81 CE, the elder son of Vespasian and the second emperor of the Flavian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titus-saint.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titus (saint)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Saint Titus was an early Christian missionary and companion of the apostle Paul, to whom the New Testament Letter to Titus is addressed.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus-didius"><span class="enc-name">Titus Didius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titus Didius was a Roman general, consul in 98 BCE and then governor of Hispania Citerior, where he campaigned against the Celtiberians for several years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus"><span class="enc-name">Titus Labienus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titus Labienus was a Roman general, Julius Caesar's most trusted legate in the Gallic Wars of 58–50 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titus-quinctius-flamininus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titus Quinctius Flamininus was a Roman general and politician who defeated Philip V of Macedon at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE, ending the Second Macedonian War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus-tatius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/titus-tatius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Titus Tatius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Titus Tatius was, in Roman legend, the king of the Sabine town of Cures who made war on Rome after the abduction of the Sabine women.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tivaradeva"><span class="enc-name">Tivaradeva</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tivaradeva was a king of the Panduvamshi dynasty of Dakshina Kosala, in modern Chhattisgarh, who reigned in the late 6th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiz"><span class="enc-name">Tiz</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tiz was a medieval port on the Makran coast of south-eastern Iran, near modern Chabahar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tizatlan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tizatlan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tizatlan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tizatlan was one of the four altepetl, or city-states, that formed the Nahua state of Tlaxcala in central Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tizoc"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tizoc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tizoc</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tizoc was the seventh ruler, or tlatoani, of the Aztec city of Tenochtitlan, reigning from 1481 to 1486.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacaelel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlacaelel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlacaelel was a Mexica statesman and adviser to several rulers of Tenochtitlan in the fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlacatecatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacatecatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlacatecatl</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The tlacatecatl was one of the highest military offices in the Aztec Empire, a senior commander of the Mexica army.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlacochcalcatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacochcalcatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlacochcalcatl</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The tlacochcalcatl was one of the two supreme military commanders of the Aztec Empire, whose title is associated with the house of darts, or armoury.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlacopan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlacopan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlacopan was a Tepanec city-state on the western shore of Lake Texcoco and the third member of the Aztec Triple Alliance, alongside Tenochtitlan and Texcoco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlalmanalco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlalmanalco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlalmanalco</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlalmanalco is a town in the State of Mexico, south-east of Mexico City near the foot of Iztaccíhuatl.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlaloc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlaloc</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlaloc was the Aztec god of rain, storms and agricultural fertility, recognisable by his goggle eyes and fangs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlapanec-people"><span class="enc-name">Tlapanec people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Tlapanecs, who call themselves Me'phaa, are an indigenous people of the mountains of eastern Guerrero, Mexico, speaking an Oto-Manguean language.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco"><span class="enc-name">Tlatelolco</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlatelolco was a Mexica city adjacent to Tenochtitlan, founded on the same island in Lake Texcoco and later absorbed into it.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlaxcalans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlaxcalans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Tlaxcalans were the people of Tlaxcala, a Nahua confederation east of the Valley of Mexico that remained independent of the Aztec Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlemcen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlemcen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Tlemcen is a city in north-western Algeria that was an important commercial and political centre of the central Maghreb.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlingit"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tlingit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Tlingit</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Tlingit are an Indigenous people of the Pacific Northwest coast, living in south-east Alaska and neighbouring British Columbia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/tlo